    body: `
<p>Filipinos use two sets of numbers every day: native Tagalog numbers and numbers borrowed from Spanish. English has one set, so the trick is knowing which set goes with which job.</p>
<h3>Native numbers 1 to 10</h3>
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>1</th><th>2</th><th>3</th><th>4</th><th>5</th></tr><tr><td class="k">isa</td><td class="k">dalawa</td><td class="k">tatlo</td><td class="k">apat</td><td class="k">lima</td></tr><tr><th>6</th><th>7</th><th>8</th><th>9</th><th>10</th></tr><tr><td class="k">anim</td><td class="k">pito</td><td class="k">walo</td><td class="k">siyam</td><td class="k">sampu</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Use these for counting things and people, for age and for how long something takes.</p>
<h3>A number needs the linker</h3>
<p>English puts the number straight before the noun: "two dogs". Tagalog joins them with the linker, exactly as with a describing word. After a vowel, add <b>-ng</b> to the number; after a consonant, put <b>na</b> in between.</p>
{{ex:isang bahay|ee-SAHNG BAH-hai|one house; a house}}
{{ex:dalawang aso|dah-lah-WAHNG AH-soh|two dogs}}
{{ex:apat na aso|AH-paht nah AH-soh|four dogs}}
{{ex:siyam na bata|shahm nah BAH-tahq|nine children}}
<p>The noun itself does not change: there is no plural ending like English -s. The number already says "more than one".</p>
<h3>Bigger numbers</h3>
<p>11 to 19 start with <b>labing-</b> or <b>labin-</b>: <b>labing-isa</b> (11), <b>labindalawa</b> (12). The tens end in <b>-pu</b>: <b>dalawampu</b> (20), <b>tatlumpu</b> (30). You will hear these less often than the Spanish ones in shops, but you will see them written. Hundreds are <b>daan</b> and thousands are <b>libo</b>, with a linked number in front.</p>
{{ex:dalawampung piso|dah-lah-wahm-POONG PEE-soh|twenty pesos}}
{{ex:isang daan|ee-SAHNG dah-AHN|one hundred}}
{{ex:dalawang daan|dah-lah-WAHNG dah-AHN|two hundred}}
<h3>Spanish numbers for prices and the clock</h3>
<p>For small prices, people usually say the Spanish number with <b>pesos</b>: <b>bente</b> (20), <b>trenta</b> (30), <b>singkwenta</b> (50). The native form is correct too, just less common in a shop. Clock time always uses Spanish numbers (<b>alas-tres</b>, three o'clock), which comes in a later lesson. So <b>tatlong oras</b> is "three hours", but <b>tres oras</b> is not Tagalog for it.</p>
{{ex:Magkano po ito?|mahg-KAH-noh poh ee-TOH|How much is this? (polite)}}
{{ex:Bente pesos po.|BEHN-teh PEH-sohs pohq|Twenty pesos. (polite)}}
{{ex:tatlong oras|taht-LOHNG OH-rahs|three hours}}
<p>Phone numbers and years are usually said in English, so you only need to recognize them.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Most common mistake:</b> leaving out the linker after a number. <i>dalawa bahay</i> → <b>dalawang bahay</b>; <i>apat aso</i> → <b>apat na aso</b>.</div>
`,
